A||||5||Scott_M||Starting problems...any ideas?||||8||
Z||000000||Scott_M||08-02-2000||07:44 PM||||I've got an 87 SE manual 4 banger. In which I've replaced the starter 1 1/2 yr ago, and it's got a brand new battery. Twice before it suddenly wouldn't start for about an hour or so. Only this time it's really being stubborn and hasn't started in a day now. I think it may be the Neutral Start Switch. I do have power but it won't crank. I've read some past posts regarding this but I need alittle more info on how to either test the current switch, or bypass it completely. The only sound made when trying to start "the little hole in the road I toss money into", comes from a "vacuum relay/switch???" on the left rear wall of the engine compartment above a black canister.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_by_Sunset.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_by_Sunset.jpg</A> <P><BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||trigger||08-02-2000||07:51 PM||||Hi,<BR>I had the same prob. with my 88 4cyl auto.  Check the big wire that goes from the battery to the solenoid and see if it is loose. Hope this helps.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-02-2000||08:28 PM||||I had the neutral start switch problem. A sign that that is the problem is the temp guage flies up with the key turned to crank, and there is no click or anything from the back.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Scott_M||08-02-2000||09:09 PM||||The thick cable is connected to the starter pretty tight. And the temp gauge does fly up when I turn the key. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||08-02-2000||10:17 PM||||you can do the screwdriver test, taking a screwdriver and connect it between the two cables and you should hear it crank.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Scott_M||08-02-2000||10:38 PM||||Which two cables? <BR>Also, what would it take to bypass the Neutral Start Switch altogether?||||reg||1
